{
  "raw": {
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/EVsqa/Research-Associate-Postdoctoral-Fellow-in-Gravitational-Microlensing,34206": {
      "title": "Research Associate/Postdoctoral Fellow in Gravitational Microlensing",
      "description": "Research Associate/Postdoctoral Fellow in Gravitational Microlensing -\r\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Research Associate/Postdoctoral Fellow in Gravitational Microlensing Te Kaupeka Pūtaiao | Faculty of Science Te Kura Matū | School of Physical and Chemical Sciences Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Full-time 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE) Fixed term position (2.25 years) Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Postdoctoral Fellow? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do The successful applicant will be expected to carry out research in collaboration with Associate Professor Michael Albrow, students, and external colleagues on the analysis of gravitational microlensing data from the NASA’s Roman Galactic Bulge Time Domain Survey (GBTDS). The main focus of the research will be to detect and characterize dark lensing objects such as free-floating planets and black holes on an individual and population level basis. The position is available to start during 2027. Mōu | Who You Are You must have a PhD in Astronomy or a closely related field. Applicants should have strong and demonstrated scientific programming and data analysis skills. Experience working with microlensing data is preferred, although applicants with expertise in other time domain astronomy or large astronomical data sets are also strongly encouraged to apply. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With The Te Kura Matū School of Physical and Chemical Sciences is a thriving community with a legacy spanning 150 years of academic excellence. Amongst the largest Schools within the University, we are driven by our collective commitment to search for innovative solutions that address the problems and challenges both within New Zealand and the World. Our School boasts a vibrant tapestry of individuals, encompassing academic, technical, and administrative professionals from a wide spectrum of backgrounds. United by our shared purpose, we are dedicated to advancing knowledge and pushing the boundaries of scientific discovery. Immersed within the Rutherford Regional Science and Innovation Centre (RRSIC), our laboratories thrive amidst state-of-the-art teaching and research infrastructure. Furthermore, our association with the broader university opens doors to other exceptional research and computational facilities, enhancing the depth of exploration. The School of Physical and Chemical Sciences offers a spectrum of majors including Astronomy, Physics, Chemistry, Biochemistry, Medicinal Chemistry, and Environmental Sciences. Our research endeavours are channelled through five central themes: Sustainability and the Environment, New Materials, Nanotechnology and Laser Physics, Energy and Storage, and Astronomy and Astrophysics, as well as Human, Animal, and Plant Health. By joining our School, you will be a part of pioneering initiatives that shape the future of scientific discovery and leave an indelible mark on the world. For more information about our team, please visit us at Physical and Chemical Sciences | University of Canterbury Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Tangata Tū, Tangata Ora - Engaged, Empowered, Making a Difference. The University of Canterbury is committed to accessible higher education, service to community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. What we offer Flexible working arrangements Supportive working environment Generous annual leave provisions and competitive salary Professional development opportunities A unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as the recreation centre and Staff club at discounted rates plus onsite café’s and eateries, and more For more information, please visit: http://www.canterbury.ac.nz/joinus For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit www.canterbury.ac.nz The closing date for this position is: 31 October 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Applications will be reviewed as received and until the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted on our careers website and should include: A cover letter detailing your motivation in applying for the position and your relevant expertise A resume Contact details for two nominated referees Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz Prospective candidates are encouraged to contact Associate Professor Michael Albrow ( michael.albrow@canterbury.ac.nz ) for further information or with any questions, please subject the email ‘Postdoctoral fellow in Gravitational Microlensing’. The salary for a Post-Doctoral Fellow for this position is NZ$89,608 p.a. The nominal start is March – September 2027 but some flexibility is possible. Please Note: Candidates who are working towards or nearing completion of a relevant Doctoral qualification, will also be considered. Upon appointment, if the Doctoral qualification is yet to be awarded, the title of Research Associate will be applied and the salary will reflect the Research Associate salary scale. Appointable salary for a Research Associate will be $74,770 p.a. For overseas applicants, please visit Immigration New Zealand website, to review the latest health and visa requirements for visiting and working in New Zealand. Job Details Reference # 34206 Posted on 07 Oct 2026 Closes on 31 Oct 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Administration, Research & Development, Science & Technology Job level(s) Entry/Graduate, Experienced Work type(s) Fixed term full-time More details (document) PD_Gravitational Microlensing.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 22 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/fJ4oa/Associate-Professor-Professor-of-Community-Nursing,33737": {
      "title": "Associate Professor/Professor of Community Nursing",
      "description": "Associate Professor/Professor of Community Nursing -\r\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Associate Professor/Professor of Community Nursing Te Kaupeka Oranga | Faculty of Health and Nurse Maude Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Full-time, 37.5 hours per week Continuing (permanent) position Generous annual leave provisions and professional development opportunities Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Pūkenga Matua / Ahorangi Tuarua? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | The University of Canterbury and Nurse Maude invite applications for a continuing joint appointment at the level of Associate Professor or Professor. Nurse Maude and UC are prominent and well-established organisations in Te Wai Pounamu (South Island – New Zealand), and this co-funded partnership reflects a shared commitment to advancing nursing as an academic discipline. By combining UC’s strengths in research and education with Nurse Maude’s longstanding expertise in high-quality community healthcare, this role creates a vital bridge between academic scholarship and clinical practice. This senior academic position has a substantial research focused component offering a unique opportunity to provide strategic research leadership and academic stewardship across two collegial, high-performing, and future-focused organisations. It reflects a joint commitment to advancing community-based healthcare, education, and research excellence in nursing. The Professorial Chair in Community Nursing/Nursing will engage with both UC and Nurse Maude, to lead initiatives that enhance research, education, and clinical practice, positioning both organisations at the forefront of innovation and evidence-informed nursing. The successful candidate will play a key role in strengthening the research profile of both the Faculty and Nurse Maude by driving a clear and ambitious research agenda aligned with local, national, and international priorities. They will bring a sustained record of research excellence, demonstrated through high-quality publications, success in securing external research funding, and the ability to lead high-performing multidisciplinary research teams. Experience translating research into clinical practice and broader impact will be essential. In addition to research leadership, the successful candidate will contribute to teaching and course development and take an active role in academic leadership through participation in Faculty and University committees. The role also involves fostering a collegial and inclusive research environment by mentoring nursing researchers across both organisations and supporting initiatives that strengthen the nursing community. The candidate will build strong relationships within UC, Nurse Maude, and beyond, while developing local, national, and international partnerships. More broadly, you will support strategic objectives by enhancing research reputation, strengthening industry engagement, and securing external funding. This appointment represents a significant opportunity for an accomplished academic leader to shape the future of nursing research, education, and practice and to contribute meaningfully to institutional, national, and global health priorities. Mōu | Who You Are We are seeking an established academic leader with a PhD in Nursing, Health or a related field.  As the Nurse Maude Professor of Nursing you will lead a research programme aligned with shared strategic priorities developed in partnership with Nurse Maude and UC. The role includes conducting and publishing high-quality research, securing external research funding, and strengthening research capability through supervision and mentoring of staff across both organisations. The Professor will also actively engage clinical staff, organisational partners, and the wider community in research and its dissemination, supporting continuous learning and the advancement of best practice. You will bring: Recognised expertise in nursing, with a strong commitment to advancing evidence-informed practice and improving health outcomes within community nursing contexts. A distinguished record of leading innovative, impactful research programmes, demonstrated by high-quality publications and sustained success in securing competitive external research funding Proven ability to build, lead, and inspire multidisciplinary research teams, and to mentor academic and clinical colleagues to develop research capability Extensive experience supervising postgraduate research students to completion, with a commitment to developing the next generation of nursing researchers An established national and/or international reputation, evidenced by leadership, collaboration, and influence within your discipline Demonstrated leadership in the design, coordination, and delivery of high-quality undergraduate and postgraduate programmes in nursing Strong, well-developed networks across healthcare, industry, government and academic sectors, with the ability to build partnerships that support research translation and impact A commitment to inclusive teaching practices and reflective professional development. A strong track record of engaging with multiple stakeholder groups to advance community nursing practice Active commitment to upholding the values, tikanga (protocols and processes), kawa (rules) of cultural practice and traditions as guided by mana whenua, Ngāi Tūāhuriri, and valuing te reo Māori Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With The Professor will be an employee of UC and also maintain a close working relationship with Nurse Maude as a strategic partner.  Te Kaupeka Oranga | Faculty of Health offers undergraduate and postgraduate programmes and research activities that respond to the dynamic nature of the health and sport sectors and their workforce. We offer undergraduate programmes in Sport and Health as well as postgraduate programmes in Counselling, Social Work, Child and Family Psychology, Nursing, Health Leadership and various population health and clinical endorsements within the Postgraduate Diploma and Master of Health Sciences. For more information on our Faculty please visit us here . Nurse Maude is a leading provider of community-based healthcare services in Aotearoa me te Waipounamu, New Zealand, with a long-standing reputation for delivering high-quality, person-centred care across Te Wai Pounamu. As an innovative and trusted organisation, Nurse Maude supports individuals and whānau to live well in their homes and communities through a wide range of services, including district nursing, specialty nursing clinics, palliative care, residential care and home support services. With a strong commitment to continuous improvement, research, and workforce development, Nurse Maude plays a vital role in shaping responsive, integrated models of care that meet the evolving needs of the communities it serves. For more information about Nurse Maude, please visit us here . Within this role, the successful candidate will engage with both Nurse Maude and UC to inform their research programme and enable alignment between the strategic objectives of each organisation. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Tangata Tū, Tangata Ora - – Engaged, Empowered, Making a Difference. We are committed to accessible higher education, service to the community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. Our values at Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha include manaakitanga, whanaungatanga and tiakitanga and these uara (values) guide our decisions and behaviour. Our values provide a roadmap for how we do things, affirming our commitment to the oranga (health, safety and wellbeing) of our students through mana-enhancing pastoral care and support. Ngā Uara (our values) challenge and inspire us to be the best we can be. They make UC a great place to work and study. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment, this includes: relocation support five weeks annual leave and five days university holidays UC Parental Leave (up to 9 weeks paid) + 26 weeks Government paid parental leave and onsite childcare facility widely accessible sabbatical provision generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) University and Faculty scholarship support for post-graduate students flexible working arrangements supportive working environment professional development and study opportunities living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as the recreation centre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information on our Benefits, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/joinus/benefits/ This role sits within the Academic Staff, Associated Staff and Academic Support Staff Collective Agreement and the following salary scale applies: Associate Professor $155,592 - $167,718 p.a., Professor $178,257 – $224.115 p.a. The closing date for this position is: 22 November 2026 (midnight NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed after the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted on our careers website and should include the following: a cover letter which includes a 2–3-page statement outlining your research interests and goals and teaching philosophy (combined) a Resume which includes a list of relevant research publications and external research funding achievements (combined). We do not accept applications by email, however, we are happy to answer any queries at WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz For further information specifically about the role, please contact Laurie McLay: laurie.mclay@canterbury.ac.nz For overseas applicants, please visit Immigration New Zealand website, to review the latest health and visa requirements for visiting and working in New Zealand. Job Details Reference # 33737 Posted on 16 Oct 2026 Closes on 22 Nov 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Associate Professor, Health, Professor Job level(s) Academic Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) Position description 1 Associate Professor_PD.pdf Position description 2 Professor_PD.pdf Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 31 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/cVqpa/Structural-Engineering-Technician,33957": {
      "title": "Structural Engineering Technician",
      "description": "Structural Engineering Technician -\r\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Structural Engineering Technician Te Tari Pūhanga Metarahi me te Taiao | Civil and Environmental Engineering Department Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand 5 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays Professional development opportunities Permanent position, 37.5 hours per week Generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Structural Engineering Technician ? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do You will provide technical and operational support across the Structural Engineering Laboratories, working with academic staff and postgraduate students to design, manufacture and test research equipment, test rigs, models, frames and laboratory specimens. You will operate and calibrate specialist testing equipment and workshop machinery, including lathes, drills, milling machines and welders, while supporting laboratory research, teaching activities and external clients. Assisting with equipment maintenance, purchasing, research budgets, project planning and ensuring laboratory activities are delivered safely, efficiently and within available resources. Mōu | Who You Are You are an experienced trades professional with strong technical skills, a practical problem-solving approach, and an interest in supporting engineering research and teaching. You will be able to demonstrate experience in: Substantial (minimum of 5 years) relevant trade experience within complex environments, including experience applying technical design knowledge and skills Advanced trade knowledge with a strong understanding of engineering concepts Hands-on experience operating workshop machinery, testing equipment and computer-controlled systems Demonstrated experience with data logging, control software, hydraulics or servo-hydraulic systems Proven commitment to health and safety, quality workmanship and professional standards Certified in the use of Forklifts, EWP and Overhead Gantry Crane is desirable Qualification – Advanced Trade Certificate, NZDE (Mech preferred) or Bachelor’s Degree (NZQA Level 7) (Mechanical/Structural Engineering preferred) or equivalent An alignment with our organisation's values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With You will work closely with the Technical Services Manager, Laboratory Manager, academic staff, postgraduate students, other technicians and external clients. Liaising with teams across the University, including Facilities Management and Security, to support the effective operation of the laboratories. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC At UC, being part of our team means more than just a job. We are committed to supporting our kaimahi | staff’s career and wellbeing with benefits designed to help our kaimahi grow and feel connected, supported and valued. 5 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangements professional development opportunities supportive working environment a wide range of retail discounts across shopping, entertainment and travel on-campus staff discounts living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as UC RecCentre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. We offer a range of benefits, for more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc . ( For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ ) The closing date for this position is: 1 November 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and resume. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency to be considered for this role. Please note, this is an onsite position. Job Details Reference # 33957 Posted on 16 Oct 2026 Closes on 01 Nov 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Engineering Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) Structural Engineering Technician_PD.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 31 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/nc2ra/Senior-Lecturer-and-Programme-Coordinator-TNE-Programmes-,34335": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer and Programme Coordinator (TNE Programmes)",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer and Programme Coordinator (TNE Programmes) -\r\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Senior Lecturer and Programme Coordinator (TNE Programmes) Te Kura Whakawhanake Hautūanga me te Ngaioanga | School of Leadership and Professional Practice Te Kaupeka Ako | Faculty of Education Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Full-time 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE) Continuing (permanent) position Generous annual leave provisions and professional development opportunities Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Pūkenga Matua? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do With the recent expansion of Faculty of Education activities in Transitional Education (TNE), we are seeking an academic with the requisite expertise for the role of Programme Coordinator for our Faculty of Education TNE programmes. This Programme Coordinator position will be based at the UC campus in Christchurch, New Zealand, The Programme Coordinator will have dual reporting lines, to both the Faculty of Education’s senior role responsible for TNE and to the assigned Head of School. Key responsibilities will include: Coordinating academic planning and delivery across the relevant TNE programmes Acting as a principal point of liaison among UC Faculty of Education, UC Global Engagement Office and partner stakeholders for Faculty of Education colleagues Coordinating programme-level timetabling, assessment, moderation and examination processes, and working with the staff responsible for these functions Supporting Faculty of Education senior leadership responsible for TNE in monitoring academic standards, student progression, and emerging programme issues Teaching on programmes in accordance with area of expertise Supporting the induction of and ongoing communication with UC staff teaching on the programmes Contributing to Joint Institute programme review, quality assurance, accreditation, and reporting requirements Identifying and escalating academic, operational, and reputational risks through the appropriate channels Participating in relevant UC and Joint Institute committees and meetings. Mōu | Who You Are You will bring evidence of effective teaching practice at Senior Lecturer level, alongside an active research or scholarship agenda in the relevant discipline. The successful applicant will provide evidence of: A PhD in Education or a closely related discipline, or your subject discipline An established track record of postdoctoral research and publication Successful experience in the co-ordination of offshore or TNE programmes involving a New Zealand university Sound knowledge of academic quality-assurance processes The ability to work effectively across institutional and cultural contexts Knowledge of the Faculty’s TNE programme offerings Strong relationship-management, organisational, and communication skills Willingness and ability to travel internationally as required. Fluency in Mandarin Chinese (Putonghua) is desirable but not essential. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With Te Kaupeka Ako | Faculty of Education is a supportive, collegial academic community with strong connections to schools, kura, early learning services, and the wider education sector. Our staff are committed to high-quality teaching, meaningful research, and professional practice that makes a difference in Aotearoa New Zealand. Academic staff benefit from opportunities for collaboration, internal research funding, professional development, and engagement across the University. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Tangata Tū, Tangata Ora - – Engaged, Empowered, Making a Difference. We are committed to accessible higher education, service to the community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. Mea e tukua nei | What we offer We offer a range of benefits, for more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc . ( For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ ) This role sits within the Academic Staff, Associated Staff and Support Staff Collective Agreement and the following salary scale applies: Senior Lecturer $119,586 - $135,630. The closing date for this position is: 28 October 2026 (midnight NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted on our careers website and should include the following: a 1-page cover letter , a 1-page research statement outlining your research interests and goals, and a 1-page teaching philosophy statement (combined) a CV which includes a list of relevant research publications (combined). Please note, only 2 documents can be uploaded in the application process. We do not accept applications by email, however, we are happy to answer any queries at WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz For overseas applicants, please visit Immigration New Zealand website, to review the latest health and visa requirements for visiting and working in New Zealand. Please note, this is an onsite position. #LI-DNI Job Details Reference # 34335 Posted on 16 Oct 2026 Closes on 28 Oct 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Education & Training, Senior Lecturer, Tertiary Teaching Job level(s) Academic, Experienced Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) Position description 1 Senior Lecturer PD.pdf Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 31 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/HL3qa/Campus-Patrol-Officer,34137": {
      "title": "Campus Patrol Officer",
      "description": "Campus Patrol Officer -\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Campus Patrol Officer Te Ratonga Pūkaha | Facilities Management Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Continuing (Permanent) Part-time position 16 hours per week (Saturday and Sunday only, 2 x 8-hour shifts) Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Campus Patrol Officer ? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do You will work weekend shifts (Saturday and Sunday only), comprising two 8-hour shifts per week (16 hours) at the Art Centre, Christchurch. As a Campus Patrol Officer, you will play a critical role in ensuring the safety and security of staff, students, visitors, and tenants within the Art Centre precinct. Your focus will be on providing security with a strong emphasis on empathy, understanding, and a people-centred approach. Patrol designated areas to maintain the safety and security of staff, students, and visitors, either on foot or via specialised vehicle. Provide escort services to staff, students, and visitors, offering assistance such as lockout services, jump-starts, and general information. Stay alert to potential threats or unauthorised persons and take appropriate action, collaborating with the Campus Security Officer (CSO) and Police as needed. Ensure building security by checking doors, windows, and gates; monitor closed buildings for unauthorised access. Conduct routine checks on emergency towers, streetlights, and security equipment, reporting any issues promptly. Assist with transportation of university employees and students as needed, using a university vehicle. Maintain clear and efficient communication within the security team and ensure appropriate use of security equipment. Respond promptly to emergencies, ensuring the safety of all individuals and following University procedures. Participate in emergency preparedness training and contribute to campus drills and exercises. Help control campus events to ensure public safety and respond to alarm activations and medical emergencies when needed. Mōu | Who You Are Empathy and Customer Service Focus: You are committed to delivering exceptional service, offering support in every interaction, whether it’s during routine patrols or emergency situations. Excellent Communication Skills: Your ability to connect with diverse people, listen actively, and provide assistance in a compassionate manner is crucial. Problem-Solving: You possess the ability to handle a variety of situations and prioritise tasks. First Aid Certified or Willing to Learn: Your ability to act quickly and calmly in emergencies is essential. Organisational Skills: You can effectively manage multiple priorities, maintain accurate logs and reports, and stay organised during high-pressure situations. Must hold a full New Zealand driver licence. A minimum of 2-3 years’ experience in security or a customer service role, preferably in a medium to large organisation. A commitment to upholding safety protocols and creating a supportive campus environment. NCEA 2/Sixth Form Certificate (NZQA Level 2) or equivalent industry experience. An alignment with our organisation's values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With UC is made up of approximately 150 buildings across 87 hectares, covering a gross floor area of 223,000m 2 The UC Security team provide a comprehensive 24-hour, 7 day a week security service across the UC campuses. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Ngā Uara | Our Values of manaakitanga, whanaungatanga and tiakitanga guide our decisions and behaviour and provide a roadmap for how we do things at UC, affirming our commitment to pastoral care and support for our ākonga and staff. They challenge and inspire us to be the best we can and make UC a great place to work and study. For more info on Ngā Uara | Our Values visit us here . We are committed to accessible higher education, service to the community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. We offer a range of fantastic benefits including flexible work practices, study opportunities and generous superannuation and leave provisions. For more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: 30 October 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and CV. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency to be considered for this role. Job Details Reference # 34137 Posted on 15 Oct 2026 Closes on 30 Oct 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Works, Trades & Services Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) part-time More details (document) PD Campus Patrol Officer.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 31 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/iggra/Workday-Change-Analyst-Impacts,34375": {
      "title": "Workday Change Analyst",
      "description": "Workday Change Analyst - Impacts -\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Workday Change Analyst - Impacts Te Ratonga Tautoko Hangarau | Digital Services Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Full-time (37.5 hours per week) Fixed term until November 2027 Āu Mahi | What You Will Do Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury is undertaking a significant transformation through the implementation of Workday. We are looking for an experienced Change Analyst to help support the implementation and adoption of Workday across university. Working within the UC Workday change management workstream, you will support the Strategic Change Manager and collaborate with staff across the University. You will focus on impacts and translate identified impacts into practical change interventions that support a smooth transition. Exceptional attention to detail is essential, as impacts that are overlooked could negatively affect user experience, business continuity and successful adoption. In this hands-on role, you will: Work with end users, functional leads and project team members to understand and document the gaps between current-state and future-state ways of working. Identify who and what will be impacted by each gap, considering people and organisation structure, technology, process, policy, information and reporting, services, location and facilities, and culture. Facilitate impact discovery and validation sessions with end users and subject-matter experts, challenging assumptions and resolving gaps or inconsistencies. Continue to develop the following artefacts: High-level Impacts by audience, summarising impacts by function and key stakeholder groups. Detailed Impact Assessments by Workday function, clearly documenting key changes, resulting impacts and affected stakeholder groups. Persona Impacts that show the cumulative impact of change on stakeholder groups, including increases or decreases in workload. Process Change slides that clearly explain changes for end users within a Workday module. Impact Plans that link priority impacts and necessary change management interventions/activities required to minimise or mitigate the impact. Identify and escalate impact-related risks, interdependencies, assumptions, decisions and information gaps that could affect readiness, adoption or business continuity. Contribute impact insights to change readiness assessments, communications and engagement, training, and go-live support. Support the transition to Workday by facilitating workshops, end-user training courses and providing post-go-live support. Capture unanticipated or emerging impacts during transition and after go-live, and recommend adjustments to change interventions and support arrangements. Mōu | Who You Are You are a pragmatic and collaborative change professional who can move comfortably between analysis, planning, facilitation and delivery. You build confidence and trust, listen carefully, communicate complex ideas clearly and understand that successful transformation depends on bringing people with you. You will bring: At least two years’ experience applying change management principles and methodologies to large-scale organisational change. Proven change management experience and the ability to assess change impacts within a complex transformation programme, preferably involving ERP implementation. Workday experience would be highly advantageous. Solid understanding of change management principles, tools and practices. Exceptional attention to detail, and the ability to manage multiple priorities. Strong analytical and critical thinking skills with the ability to assess change impacts and develop effective strategies. Excellent written and verbal communication and interpersonal skills to engage and influence stakeholders. Strong workshop facilitation, interviewing and stakeholder engagement skills. A proven ability to build effective relationships and work with people at all levels of a complex organisation. The ability to work independently while contributing positively to a collaborative team. A relevant bachelor’s degree, ideally in management, information technology, education or a related field. A Prosci Change Management certification and experience in tertiary education would be desirable. Competency in te reo Māori me ōna tikanga would also be an advantage. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Ngā Uara | Our Values of manaakitanga, whanaungatanga and tiakitanga guide our decisions and behaviour and provide a roadmap for how we do things at UC, affirming our commitment to pastoral care and support for our ākonga and staff. They challenge and inspire us to be the best we can, and make UC a great place to work and study. For more info on Ngā Uara | Our Values visit us here . We are committed to accessible higher education, service to the community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: 1 st November 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and resume. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency or hold a valid NZ work visa to be considered for this role. Job Details Reference # 34375 Posted on 15 Oct 2026 Closes on 01 Nov 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Information Technology Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Fixed term full-time More details (document) PD Workday Change Analyst.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 31 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/nMjqa/Kaiarataki-Karahipi-M-ori-Scholarship-Lead,34182": {
      "title": "Kaiarataki Karahipi",
      "description": "Kaiarataki Karahipi | Māori Scholarship Lead -\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Kaiarataki Karahipi | Māori Scholarship Lead Office of Treaty Partnership | UC Māori Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand 5 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays Professional development opportunities Permanent position, 22.5 hours per week Generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He tūranga hou tēnei e tautoko ana I te angitu o ngā ākonga Māori mā ngā karahipi me ngā Kaupapa tautoko. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do This is This is an exciting opportunity to provide operational leadership for UC Māori scholarship programmes and help shape initiatives that support ākonga Māori to thrive at Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury. As Kaiarataki Karahipi | Māori Scholarship Lead, you will lead the planning, development and implementation of the Tūāpapa and Kaitoko Māori scholarship programmes. You will coordinate recruitment and selection, scholarship activities and wrap-around academic, social and cultural support, while working collaboratively across the Scholarships Office, Faculties and Service Units. You will also identify opportunities to strengthen scholarship access, funding pathways, academic development and student engagement across the Māori student journey, with a particular focus on successful transition and first-year success. Mōu | Who You Are To be successful in this role you will hold a bachelor’s degree (NZQA Level 7), with a minimum of four years’ experience in a related role and evidence of: Demonstrated experience in professional coordination, administration and influencing professionally qualified staff. Knowledge or understanding of project management, marketing and/or strategic planning. Substantial experience working with diverse students or customers, particularly Māori students, whānau and communities. Excellent oral and written communication skills, including active listening, mentoring and coaching capability. Proven experience implementing change, contributing to strategic initiatives and working collaboratively to achieve team outcomes. Demonstrated commitment to cultural awareness and competence, UC values, tikanga, kawa, traditions guided by mana whenua, and valuing te reo Māori. Previous experience in a tertiary institution, with competency in te reo Māori me ōna tikanga an advantage. An alignment with our organisation's values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With You will work as part of UC Māori and collaborate closely with the Scholarships Office, Faculties and Service Units across the University. You will build strong relationships with ākonga Māori and connect with relevant outreach programmes, communities and UC services to support recruitment, transition, engagement and success. The role will also identify and coordinate appropriate academic support, including supplementary instruction and peer tutoring, to ensure ākonga can access and engage with the support they need. Ko tā UC Māori mahi ko te ārahi i Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha whānui, ka whakakahatia te māiatanga, me te āheitanga o Te Tiriti o Waitangi hei toitū te tiriti. The role of UC Māori is to provide leadership, support and guidance across the University in strengthening Te Tiriti o Waitangi competence and confidence and supporting the University to uphold its responsibilities as a Treaty partner. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC At UC, being part of our team means more than just a job. We are committed to supporting our kaimahi | staff’s career and wellbeing with benefits designed to help our kaimahi grow and feel connected, supported and valued. 5 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangements professional development opportunities supportive working environment a wide range of retail discounts across shopping, entertainment and travel on-campus staff discounts living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as UC RecCentre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: 19 October 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and resume. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency to be considered for this role. Please note, this is an onsite position. #LI-DNI Job Details Reference # 34182 Posted on 14 Oct 2026 Closes on 19 Oct 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Education & Training, Te Ao Māori Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) part-time More details (document) PD Kaiarataki Karahipi.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 31 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/rrjoa/Lecturer-Fine-Arts-Painting-,33763": {
      "title": "Lecturer",
      "description": "Lecturer - Fine Arts (Painting) -\r\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Lecturer - Fine Arts (Painting) Mātai Kōwaiwai | Fine Arts Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Full-time 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE) Continuing (permanent) position Generous annual leave provisions and professional development opportunities Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Pūkenga ? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do UC's Ilam School of Fine Arts is looking for an experienced academic/artist to be a Lecturer in Painting. In this position, you will deliver high-quality teaching to our painting students, supervise Honours, Masters, and practice-based PhD students, while producing high calibre research outputs and assisting with administrative duties within the school. You will also help ensure that academic programmes remain current, impactful, and responsive to emerging needs in the field. This appointment represents a significant opportunity for an emerging or more established academic to shape the direction of research and education within the school and the wider field. Mōu | Who You Are To be successful in this position, you will be active in research/building your outputs/portfolio, scholarship and creative work. You will also have the following: A PhD in a relevant field is preferred, however applicants with a Master's Degree or equivalent (NZQA Level 9 or above), preferably a Master of Fine Arts would be considered A dynamic understanding of contemporary art practice, with a deep knowledge of contemporary painting practice and its historical antecedents An appreciation of the cultural and artistic contexts of Aotearoa/New Zealand and how these relate to a global arts network/discourse A commitment to inclusive teaching practices and reflective professional development Active commitment to upholding the values, tikanga (protocols and processes), kawa (rules) of cultural practice and traditions as guided by mana whenua, Ngāi Tūāhuriri, and valuing te reo Māori An ability to discuss, practice and operate across a diverse set of art practices/disciplines. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With The Ilam School of Fine Arts offers a boutique-style education in both the practical field of fine arts and the wider liberal arts. The ability to specialise in one of five disciplines; design, moving image, painting, photography and sculpture, is unique within New Zealand. Staff are all practicing artists, designers, filmmakers and curators with established reputations. Our recent graduates join a long list of highly regarded alumni who continue to make significant artistic, cultural and social differences in New Zealand and the world beyond Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Tangata Tū, Tangata Ora - – Engaged, Empowered, Making a Difference. We are committed to accessible higher education, service to the community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. Mea e tukua nei | What we offer The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment, this includes: relocation support five weeks annual leave and five days university holidays UC Parental Leave (up to 9 weeks paid) + 26 weeks Government paid parental leave and onsite childcare facility widely accessible sabbatical provision generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) University and Faculty scholarship support for post-graduate students a unique bequest funded endowment ( Erskine Fund ) that supports an extensive visiting fellowship programme flexible working arrangements supportive working environment professional development and study opportunities living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as the recreation centre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information on our Benefits, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/joinus/benefits/ This role sits within the Academic Staff, Associated Staff and Support Staff Collective Agreement and the following salary scale applies: Lecturer $94,181 p.a.(2.01) - $106,616p.a. (2.04). The closing date for this position is: 8 November 2026 (midnight NZ time) Please note, applications may be reviewed before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted online through our careers website and should include: a 'cover letter' including a 2–3-page statement outlining your research history, interests and goals, teaching philosophy, and your thoughts on how the School of Fine Arts can continue to grow and develop over the coming years. a 'resume' which includes a list of relevant research-led and research-based practice. We do not accept applications by email, however, we are happy to answer any queries at WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz For further information specifically about the role, please contact: Aaron Kreisler, Head of Creative and Digital Arts, aaron.kreisler@canterbury.ac.nz For overseas applicants, please visit Immigration New Zealand website, to review the latest health and visa requirements for visiting and working in New Zealand. Please note, this is an onsite position. Job Details Reference # 33763 Posted on 14 Oct 2026 Closes on 08 Nov 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Lecturer Job level(s) Academic Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) Position description 1 PD_Lecturer in Fine Arts (Painting).pdf Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 31 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/yanpa/Future-Student-Advisor-Auckland,33952": {
      "title": "Future Student Advisor",
      "description": "Future Student Advisor - Auckland -\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Future Student Advisor - Auckland Te Rōpū Pāpori | Engagement Located in Tāmaki Makaurau | Auckland, Aotearoa | New Zealand Permanent position, 37.5 hours per week 5 weeks annual leave + 5 days university holidays Generous superannuation provisions (up to 6.75% employer contribution) Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Future Student Advisor? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do Want to inspire the next generation of university students? Enjoy meeting people, building relationships and turning genuine interest into action? This is an opportunity to put your sales, presentation and relationship-building skills to work in a role where you can influence students’ decisions about their future. Based in our co-working office space in Tāmaki Makaurau | Auckland, you will be part of the Future Students team, developing and delivering recruitment activity across your allocated markets, schools, regions and organisations. This role involves building meaningful connections with future students and key stakeholders, bringing the UC experience to life through engaging presentations and events, and using market insight and recruitment data to focus activity where it can have the greatest impact. Drive recruitment and conversion against agreed targets, managing a portfolio and sales pipeline to turn student interest into applications, accepted offers and enrolments. Build and nurture strong relationships with future students, parents, whānau, schools and other key stakeholders, guiding them through their journey towards UC. Bring a consultative sales approach to conversations, understanding what matters to future students and confidently communicating the value of studying at UC. Plan and deliver engaging presentations, school visits, events and other recruitment activities for diverse audiences. Use CRM information, market trends, recruitment outcomes and feedback to identify opportunities, focus activity and strengthen future recruitment strategies. Provide accurate and engaging advice on UC programmes, admission, entry and pathway requirements. Work collaboratively across faculties and service areas to ensure UC is represented accurately, consistently and with pride. Represent UC across Aotearoa, with domestic travel required as part of your recruitment and stakeholder activity. Mōu | Who You Are You are a confident and outgoing relationship builder who enjoys being in front of people, creating connections and achieving results. You bring a genuine interest in helping future students make important decisions, combined with the commercial confidence to influence conversations, identify opportunities and turn interest into action. Professionally confident and self-directed, you are comfortable working independently, managing your own portfolio and taking ownership of your priorities. You will also be able to demonstrate: 2–3 years’ experience in sales, marketing, recruitment, conversion, account management, advisory or another relevant relationship-focused environment. Experience in tertiary or secondary education, with an understanding of the sector, is desirable. Strong presentation, communication and influencing skills, with confidence engaging diverse audiences. A proven ability to build and maintain productive relationships and work effectively with people from different backgrounds. A results-focused approach, with the resilience and persistence to follow opportunities through. Experience using data, reporting and/or CRM systems to manage activity, identify trends and inform decisions. A bachelor’s degree or equivalent relevant qualification, the ability to travel within New Zealand and a full New Zealand driver licence. An alignment with our organisation's values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With You will join the Future Students team, working alongside colleagues across domestic and international student recruitment to build the profile and reputation of UC as an outstanding destination for study. Based in our co-working space in Parnell, you will work closely with another Auckland-based Future Student Advisor, collaborating to deliver shared outcomes while remaining connected to the wider Future Students team. The role connects you with a wide range of people across the University and beyond — from future students, parents and whānau to schools, faculties and service areas. Together, you will create positive recruitment experiences and ensure UC’s programmes, opportunities and student experience are represented with accuracy, enthusiasm and pride. For more information about our team, please visit us here . Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC At UC, being part of our team means more than just a job. We are committed to supporting our kaimahi | staff’s career and wellbeing with benefits designed to help our kaimahi grow and feel connected, supported and valued. 5 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangements professional development opportunities supportive working environment a wide range of retail discounts across shopping, entertainment and travel A great Auckland office environment – enjoy working from our modern co-working space, complete with unlimited coffee, a well-stocked chocolate jar and great company! For more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: 28 October 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and CV. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency or hold a valid NZ work visa to be considered for this role. Job Details Reference # 33952 Posted on 14 Oct 2026 Closes on 28 Oct 2026 23:55 Location(s) Auckland Expertise Advertising, Arts & Media, Communications, Education & Training Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) PD_Future Student Advisor.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 31 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/Rupqa/Student-Advisor-Law,34201": {
      "title": "Student Advisor, Law",
      "description": "Student Advisor, Law -\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Student Advisor, Law Te Kaupeka Ture | Faculty of Law Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Part-time (18.75 hours per week) Permanent (continuing) position Generous annual leave provisions and professional development opportunities Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Kaitoko? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do The University of Canterbury places its students first and strives to offer every student a ‘world class learning environment’ that promotes individual growth and development. The Faculty of Law supports its students and graduates to not only achieve academically but also to develop attributes related to employability and entrepreneurship, bicultural confidence, global awareness and engagement with the community. Reporting to the Academic Services Manager, the Student Advisor is responsible for the provision of advice and assistance to students relating to degree programmes offered by the Faculty of Law. You will support student development through the provision of individualised support and engagement programmes including monitoring student performance. You will support the Executive Dean of Law and Academic Services Manager in activities related to enrolment, admissions, student-related administration, marketing and outreach. Mōu | Who You Are You will have 3–4 years’ experience in a similar role, preferably in tertiary education environment and hold a Bachelor’s Degree (NZQA Level 7), as well as have: Experience of working with students in tertiary study. Highly developed written and oral communication skills High competence in MS Office suite, CRM and email technologies and databases. Experience in managing a diverse workload, and at times working to tight deadlines. Knowledge of University regulations, interpretation and application of policies and procedures. An alignment with our organisation's values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With The Faculty of Law has a reputation for excellence in teaching, research and professional contribution. Programmes offered by the Law School include the LLB, Bachelor of Criminal Justice (BCJ), Graduate options for both law and criminal justice as well as Postgrad options for example the LLM in International Law and Politics (LLM ILAP), LLM by thesis and Masters in Criminal Justice (MCJ).  The Law School also has a vibrant research student programme. For more information about our team, please visit us @ Faculty of Law Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Ngā Uara | Our Values of manaakitanga, whanaungatanga and tiakitanga guide our decisions and behaviour and provide a roadmap for how we do things at UC, affirming our commitment to pastoral care and support for our ākonga and staff. They challenge and inspire us to be the best we can, and make UC a great place to work and study. For more info on Ngā Uara | Our Values visit us here . We are committed to accessible higher education, service to the community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. We offer a range of fantastic benefits including flexible work practices, study opportunities and generous superannuation and leave provisions. For more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc . For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: Monday, 26 October 2025 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and resume. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency to be considered for this role. Job Details Reference # 34201 Posted on 12 Oct 2026 Closes on 26 Oct 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Education & Training, Student Services Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) part-time More details (document) PD Student Advisor.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 31 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/5pjqa/Senior-Tutor-Game-Arts-and-Animation,34181": {
      "title": "Senior Tutor",
      "description": "Senior Tutor - Game Arts and Animation -\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Senior Tutor - Game Arts and Animation Te Kaupeka Pūhanga | Faculty of Engineering Kura Hanga Otinga | School of Product Design Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand 4 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays Fixed term position until 17 December 2027, 37.5 hours per week Generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Kaiwhakaako Matua? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do The School of Product Design | Kura Hanga Otinga is seeking a motivated and collaborative fixed-term Senior Tutor to contribute to high-quality teaching and learning within our undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. As a Senior Tutor, you will bring disciplinary expertise and a developing pedagogical practice to create a stimulating, research-informed, and supportive learning environment. You will deliver teaching across a range of courses, support student learning and assessment, and contribute to the broader academic and service activities of the School. Key responsibilities include: delivering high-quality teaching using innovative, inclusive, and effective pedagogical practices supporting programme delivery through tutorials, studios, workshops, and assessment activities designing and delivering assessments and providing timely, constructive feedback to students embedding the UC Graduate Profile and encouraging critical, analytical, and creative thinking actively engaging in course evaluation, reflective practice, and continuous improvement contributing to service and administrative activities that support School, Faculty, and University objectives Mōu | Who You Are You are an enthusiastic educator with expertise in Game Art and Animation pipelines and a strong commitment to teaching excellence. You are developing your academic career and pedagogical practice, and you enjoy working collaboratively within a creative and collegial environment. You will demonstrate an active commitment to bicultural confidence and competence, including upholding tikanga and values as guided by mana whenua, Ngāi Tūāhuriri, and valuing te reo Māori. Applicants will demonstrate: Evidence of a growing reputation or expertise within a relevant discipline area Experience teaching Game Arts and Animation pipelines—including 2D and 3D workflows within static and real-time rendering environments with version control using GitHub—through effective, inclusive methods that support a diverse range of learners. A commitment to reflective practice and ongoing professional development Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to work collaboratively with students and colleagues Competence in digital tools and learning technologies relevant to tertiary teaching Experience with on-line and remote-study course design and deliver technologies An active commitment to UC values, inclusivity, and Te Tiriti o Waitangi Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With The School of Product Design is part of the University of Canterbury’s vibrant academic community and contributes to teaching, research, and innovation that responds to real-world challenges. You will work closely with academic and professional staff within the School and Faculty, contributing to a supportive, collaborative culture that enhances student success and the University’s reputation. For more information, please visit here . Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Tangata Tū, Tangata Ora - – Engaged, Empowered, Making a Difference. We are committed to accessible higher education, service to the community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. Mea e tukua nei | What we offer At UC, being part of our team means more than just a job. We are committed to supporting our kaimahi | staff’s career and wellbeing with benefits designed to help our kaimahi grow and feel connected, supported and valued. 4 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangement professional development opportunities supportive working environment a wide range of retail discounts across shopping, entertainment and travel on-campus staff discounts living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as UC RecCentre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: 10 November 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and resume. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency or hold a valid NZ work visa to be considered for this role. Please note, this is an onsite position. Job Details Reference # 34181 Posted on 27 Oct 2026 Closes on 10 Nov 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Design & Architecture, Education & Training, Tertiary Teaching Job level(s) Academic Work type(s) Fixed term full-time More details (document) Position description 1 Senior Tutor_PD.pdf Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 30 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/8VEqa/Purchasing-Support-Administrator,34243": {
      "title": "Purchasing Support Administrator",
      "description": "Purchasing Support Administrator -\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Purchasing Support Administrator Te Ratonga Ahumoni | Financial Services Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Permanent position, 37.5 hours per week 5 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays Generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Purchasing Support Administrator? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do Are you a detail-oriented administrator who enjoys working with financial systems, solving problems and making processes run smoothly? Join UC’s Financial Services team and play a key role in keeping purchasing and expense management operations accurate, efficient and well supported across the University. From administering purchasing systems to supporting P-Card and expense processes, your work will help staff navigate systems confidently and ensure purchasing activities meet UC’s policies and requirements. Bring your analytical mindset to reporting, data quality and process improvement, while contributing to the transition to Workday and the ongoing enhancement of purchasing systems and services. Mōu | Who You Are You are an organised and solutions-focused professional who combines strong administrative skills with a commitment to accuracy, responsive service and continuous improvement. You will bring: Proven experience in purchasing, finance, accounts payable, procure-to-pay or a similar administrative environment. Ideally with 4-5 years in a similar role. Experience administering financial, purchasing, expense management or enterprise systems, with a sound understanding of purchasing processes and controls. Strong digital capability, including Microsoft Excel, Word and other business applications, alongside experience preparing reports and analysing operational information. Excellent attention to detail and the ability to manage high volumes of information, identify discrepancies and maintain accurate records. Strong communication and customer service skills, with the confidence to support users, explain processes and work collaboratively with diverse stakeholders. The ability to prioritise competing demands, resolve issues and contribute to process improvements while maintaining service standards. Experience in supplier administration, financial records, or staff training is an advantage. An alignment with our organisation's values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With Working within Financial Services, you will collaborate closely with colleagues across Procurement and Finance, as well as Digital Services and departments throughout UC. Through responsive support and practical guidance, you will help staff use purchasing systems effectively and maintain reliable supplier and expense processes — building strong working relationships while contributing to a more efficient, consistent purchasing service. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC At UC, being part of our team means more than just a job. We are committed to supporting our kaimahi | staff’s career and wellbeing with benefits designed to help our kaimahi grow and feel connected, supported and valued. 5 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays UC Parental Leave (up to 9 weeks paid for eligible staff) + 26 weeks Government paid parental leave and onsite childcare facility generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangements professional development opportunities supportive working environment a wide range of retail discounts across shopping, entertainment and travel on-campus staff discounts living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as UC RecCentre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: 10 November 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and CV. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency to be considered for this role. [Choose continuing roles] Please note, this is an onsite position. Job Details Reference # 34243 Posted on 27 Oct 2026 Closes on 10 Nov 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Administration, Finance, Procurement Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) Purchasing Support Administrator_PD.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 30 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
