    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/3Gvqa/Personal-Assistant,34215": {
      "title": "Personal Assistant",
      "description": "Personal Assistant -\r\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Personal Assistant Te Rōpū Pāpori | Engagement Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand 5 weeks annual leave + 5 days university holidays Professional development opportunities Permanent position, 37.5 hours per week Generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) Āu Mahi | What You Will Do This is an opportunity to work at the centre of a broad, high-impact portfolio that shapes how Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury (UC) connects with its communities, partners, supporters and future students. As Personal Assistant to the Assistant Vice-Chancellor Engagement (AVCE), you will be a trusted professional providing proactive, confidential and highly professional support to this senior leader across a portfolio spanning advancement, communications, marketing and external relations, domestic and international student recruitment, student operations, and global engagement. In this varied role, you will: manage a complex calendar and email flow, anticipate priorities and coordinate the information needed for meetings, appointments and decisions prepare agendas, briefing material, presentations and minutes, and follow up actions to keep commitments and deadlines on track handle sensitive and confidential information with sound judgement, discretion and integrity coordinate travel, events and hospitality, including arrangements involving senior leaders and external stakeholders build effective relationships across the Engagement portfolio, the wider University, and with external partners collate information from multiple sources and present it clearly and usefully for the AVCE support financial administration, including invoices, expenses, purchasing and PCard processes contribute ideas that improve administration, workflow and the effectiveness of the Engagement Office Mōu | Who You Are You are an accomplished Personal Assistant or senior administrator who enjoys creating order, building trusted relationships and enabling a senior leader to perform at their best. You bring calm professionalism to competing demands, notice what needs to happen next, and take ownership of seeing it through. You will also be able to demonstrate: 4–6 years’ experience in a high-level administrative support role, preferably in a large or complex organisation excellent calendar, email, workflow and time management skills across multiple priorities strong written and verbal communication, with confidence working with senior leaders and a wide range of internal and external stakeholders high-quality minute taking, document preparation and information-management skills excellent attention to detail, problem-solving ability and a strong customer focus advanced capability with Microsoft 365 applications, including Outlook, Word, Excel, PowerPoint and Teams, and confidence learning new systems experience with financial administration and purchasing processes; Flexipurchase and Orbit experience would be an advantage the ability to work independently while contributing positively to a collaborative administrative network unwavering discretion, integrity and respect for confidentiality An alignment with our organisation's values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With You will report directly to the Assistant Vice-Chancellor Engagement and be based in the Engagement Office. You will work closely with leaders and colleagues across Advancement, Communications, Marketing and External Relations, Student Recruitment, Student Operations, and International Relations and Partnerships. You will also connect with the Vice-Chancellor’s Office, other Personal Assistants and administrators across UC, and a diverse range of community, industry, alumni, donor, mana whenua and international stakeholders. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC At UC, being part of our team means more than just a job. We are committed to supporting our kaimahi | staff’s career and wellbeing with benefits designed to help our kaimahi grow and feel connected, supported and valued. 5 weeks annual leave + 5 days university holidays generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangements professional development opportunities a supportive and inclusive working environment a wide range of retail discounts across shopping, entertainment and travel on-campus staff discounts living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment on a beautiful campus, with access to UC facilities such as the UC RecCentre and Staff Club at discounted rates, plus onsite cafés and eateries For more information about benefits, please visit: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: 3 November 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and CV. Please note, we do not accept applications by email. However, we are happy to answer queries about the application process; please email WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz. You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency to be considered for this role. Please note, this is an onsite position. Job Details Reference # 34215 Posted on 20 Oct 2026 Closes on 03 Nov 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Administration, Communications Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) PD_Personal Assistant.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 30 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/xLvra/Workday-Change-Analyst-Stakeholders,34411": {
      "title": "Workday Change Analyst",
      "description": "Workday Change Analyst - Stakeholders -\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Workday Change Analyst - Stakeholders Te Ratonga Tautoko Hangarau | Digital Services Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Full-time (37.5 hours per week) Fixed term until November 2027 Āu Mahi | What You Will Do Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury is undertaking a significant transformation through the implementation of Workday. We are looking for an experienced Change Analyst to help support the implementation and adoption of Workday across university. Working within the UC Workday change management workstream, you will support the Strategic Change Manager and collaborate with staff across the University. You will focus on stakeholder management and ensure the right people are engaged and receive the right information, at the right time, and in the right way. In this hands-on role, you will: Support the Strategic Change Manager to develop and execute change management plans, ensuring alignment with the University’s goals for this project. Conduct stakeholder analysis and maintain a schedule to ensure stakeholders, including a change network, are regularly engaged. Create stakeholder personas and change journeys. Create communication and engagement activities to promote awareness and understanding of Workday changes among employees. Facilitate workshops with end users to increase understanding of how Workday will impact them and their teams. Actively manage a change network to build advocacy, adoption, and capability across UC. Conduct change readiness assessments and identify areas for attention. Create post go-live support systems for end users. Help monitor and measure the effectiveness of change management initiatives, providing feedback and recommendations for improvement. Create training material where required. Support the transition to Workday by facilitating end-user training courses and providing post-go-live support. Capture unanticipated or emerging impacts during transition and after go-live, and recommend adjustments to change interventions and support arrangements. Mōu | Who You Are You are a pragmatic and collaborative change professional who can move comfortably between analysis, planning, facilitation and delivery. You build confidence and trust, listen carefully, communicate complex ideas clearly and understand that successful transformation depends on bringing people with you. You will bring: At least two years’ experience applying change management principles and methodologies to large-scale organisational change. Proven change management experience and the ability to collaborate with various stakeholders within a complex transformation programme, preferably involving ERP implementation. Workday experience would be highly advantageous. Solid understanding of change management principles, tools and practices. Exceptional attention to detail, and the ability to manage multiple priorities. Strong analytical skills with the ability to assess change impacts on stakeholders. Excellent written and verbal communication and interpersonal skills to engage and influence stakeholders. Strong workshop facilitation, interviewing and stakeholder engagement skills. A proven ability to build effective relationships and work with people at all levels of a complex organisation. The ability to work independently while contributing positively to a collaborative team. A relevant bachelor’s degree, ideally in management, information technology, education or a related field. A Prosci Change Management certification and experience in tertiary education would be desirable. Competency in te reo Māori me ōna tikanga would also be an advantage. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC Ngā Uara | Our Values of manaakitanga, whanaungatanga and tiakitanga guide our decisions and behaviour and provide a roadmap for how we do things at UC, affirming our commitment to pastoral care and support for our ākonga and staff. They challenge and inspire us to be the best we can, and make UC a great place to work and study. For more info on Ngā Uara | Our Values visit us here . We are committed to accessible higher education, service to the community and the encouragement of talent without barriers of distance, wealth, class, gender or ethnicity. The University explicitly aims to produce graduates and support staff who are engaged with their communities, empowered to act for good and determined to make a difference in the world. For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: 2 November 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and resume. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency or hold a valid NZ work visa to be considered for this role. Job Details Reference # 34411 Posted on 19 Oct 2026 Closes on 02 Nov 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Information Technology Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Fixed term full-time More details (document) PD Workday Change Analyst.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 30 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/TEgqa/Confucius-Institute-Coordinator,34175": {
      "title": "Confucius Institute Coordinator",
      "description": "Confucius Institute Coordinator -\r\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Confucius Institute Coordinator Confucius Institute Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand 5 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays Professional development opportunities Permanent position, 37.5 hours per week Generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Confucius Institute Coordinator? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do Be part of bringing Chinese language and culture to communities across the South Island. As Confucius Institute Coordinator, you will connect schools, communities and the University of Canterbury through engaging language programmes and cultural experiences, helping build meaningful relationships, strengthen cross-cultural understanding and inspire a lasting appreciation of Chinese culture. From coordinating the Mandarin Language Assistant programme and supporting assistants as they settle into life in New Zealand, to organising school camps in China, cultural events and Chinese prizegivings, you will bring people, logistics and communications together to make things happen. This role involves overseeing day-to-day office administration, course and event promotion, digital content and financial processes, helping ensure the Confucius Institute runs smoothly and delivers a positive experience for its diverse communities. Mōu | Who You Are You are a proactive and organised coordinator who enjoys working with people from different cultural backgrounds and balancing a varied workload. You can take ownership of tasks and priorities while contributing positively to a collaborative team. You will bring: 4–5 years’ experience in general office administration Strong organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities, maintain accuracy and meet deadlines. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with fluency in English. Mandarin language skills are preferred. A culturally sensitive and inclusive approach, with an interest in Chinese culture and a willingness to learn. Maintaining confidentiality, using sound judgement and providing responsive, professional support to a range of stakeholders. A tertiary qualification equivalent to National Certificate (NZQA Level 4). Competency in te reo Māori me ōna tikanga is an advantage. Willingness and ability to travel within New Zealand and to China, if required. An alignment with our organisation's values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment. The successful candidate will hold a full, current New Zealand driver’s licence and a current First Aid Certificate or be willing to undertake First Aid training. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With Working with the Confucius Institute Directors, UC colleagues and partners across the South Island and China, you will build relationships with schools, Mandarin Language Assistants and local communities to support the Institute’s programmes. For more information about our institute, please visit us at Confucius Institute Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC At UC, being part of our team means more than just a job. We are committed to supporting our kaimahi | staff’s career and wellbeing with benefits designed to help our kaimahi grow and feel connected, supported and valued. 5 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangements professional development opportunities supportive working environment a wide range of retail discounts across shopping, entertainment and travel on-campus staff discounts living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as UC RecCentre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: 2 November 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and CV. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency to be considered for this role. Please note, this is an onsite position. Job Details Reference # 34175 Posted on 19 Oct 2026 Closes on 02 Nov 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Administration Job level(s) Experienced Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) full-time More details (document) Confucius Institute Coordinator_PD.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 30 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
