    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/AkOra/Admissions-Officer,34458": {
      "title": "Admissions Officer",
      "description": "Admissions Officer -\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Admissions Officer Te Ratonga Ākonga | Student Operations Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand Full-time 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE) Fixed term position till December 2028 4 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays Generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Admissions Officer ? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do As an Admissions Officer, you will help future students take the next step towards university study by assessing domestic and international applications, interpreting admission requirements, and providing accurate advice to applicants and stakeholders. Working as part of a collaborative team, you will support informed admission decisions and contribute to a positive student experience. This is a varied and rewarding role for a motivated individual who enjoys working in a dynamic environment. Mōu | Who You Are You are a detail oriented and customer focused professional with strong research, communication and problem-solving skills. You can interpret complex information, manage competing priorities and make sound, accurate decisions. To be successful in this role, you will have: Proven experience in administration, preferably within a tertiary education or similar environment. Experience assessing applications, qualifications, or documentation against established requirements. Demonstrated ability to interpret and apply policies, regulations, and procedures. Strong research and analytical skills, with the ability to investigate issues and support decision-making. Experience using student management systems and Microsoft Office applications. Strong written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to engage effectively with a range of stakeholders. Proven ability to manage deadlines, maintain accurate and confidential records, and contribute to team success. An alignment with our organisation's values and culture, promoting a positive and inclusive work environment. A bachelor’s degree at NZQA Level 7 or equivalent is required. Knowledge of tertiary qualifications, international education systems, te reo Māori and tikanga would be advantageous. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With We are a highly collaborative team dedicated to delivering an exceptional student experience. With students at the centre of everything we do, we work together to create a positive, inclusive, and supportive environment where every student can thrive. In this role, you will work closely with the Admissions Team Leader and wider Admissions team, as well as academic and professional staff, the Future Students Tema, Scholarships teams, University agents, applicants, and external education stakeholders. Through these partnerships, you will help deliver a consistent, efficient, and student-centred admissions experience while supporting prospective students on their journey to UC. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC At UC, being part of our team means more than just a job. We are committed to supporting our kaimahi | staff’s career and wellbeing with benefits designed to help our kaimahi grow and feel connected, supported and valued. 4 weeks annual leave +5 days university holidays generous employer contribution to superannuation (up to 6.75%) flexible working arrangement professional development opportunities supportive working environment a wide range of retail discounts across shopping, entertainment and travel on-campus staff discounts living in revitalised Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa New Zealand a unique working environment in a beautiful campus with access to UC facilities such as UC RecCentre and Staff Club at discounted rates plus onsite cafés and eateries, and more. For more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ The closing date for this position is: 13 November 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Please note, applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted via our careers website and should include a cover letter and resume. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency or hold a valid NZ work visa to be considered for this role. Please note, this is an onsite position. Job Details Reference # 34458 Posted on 30 Oct 2026 Closes on 13 Nov 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Administration, Student Services Job level(s) Entry/Graduate, Experienced Work type(s) Fixed term full-time More details (document) PD Admissions Officer.pdf Position description 1 Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 28 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    },
    "https://jobs.canterbury.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/wEfpa/Lecturer-Teaching-and-Administration-,33919": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching and Administration)",
      "description": "Lecturer (Teaching and Administration) -\r\n        University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search Lecturer (Teaching and Administration) Aotahi | School of Māori and Indigenous Studies Located in Ōtautahi | Christchurch, Aotearoa | New Zealand 18.75 hours per week (0.5 FTE) Continuing (permanent) position Generous annual leave provisions and professional development opportunities Kia hiwa rā, kia hiwa rā! He hiahia, he pūkenga nōu ki te mahi a te Pūkenga? Nāia te pōwhiri nā Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha kia tono mai i te tūranga nei. Āu Mahi | What You Will Do The University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha invites applications for a continuing Lecturer (Teaching and Administration) in Aotahi School of Māori and Indigenous Studies. You will contribute to teaching across Aotahi, bringing expertise in one or more areas of Māori and Indigenous development, including Māori organisations and leadership, policy, and Te Tiriti o Waitangi, social development and collective wellbeing, and Māori cultural and social identity. You will design and deliver high-quality teaching and assessment, support a wide range of learners to succeed, contribute to curriculum and programme development and take an active role in the wider academic and administrative life of the school. Mōu | Who You Are To be successful in this position you will ideally have the following: Honours Degree, master’s degree, or current PhD candidate in a relevant discipline, or equivalent combination of relevant academic and professional experience. Demonstrated understanding of, and experience in, delivering effective teaching that enables a wide range of learners to succeed. Evidence of reputation within your discipline. Teaching, research and/or professional expertise in one or more areas relevant to Māori and Indigenous development. Experience working with Māori communities, organisations, iwi, hapū and/or Māori and Indigenous development organisations, with the ability to connect practical experience to theory, research and engaging teaching. Relevant experience in areas such as Māori policy, governance, kaupapa Māori research and evaluation, and the development of research, evaluation reports, publications or other scholarly outputs. Evidence of reflective learning and continuous professional development. An active commitment to upholding the values, tikanga and kawa of cultural practice and traditions as guided by mana whenua, Ngāi Tūāhuriri, and to valuing te reo Māori. Competency in te reo Māori me ōna tikanga is an advantage. Mahi Ngātahi | Who You Will Work With Aotahi School of Māori and Indigenous Studies is part of Te Kura Toi | Faculty of Arts at Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury. Aotahi brings together teaching, research and engagement across te reo Māori, Māori and Indigenous knowledge, development, culture and society. You will join a collegial team committed to high-quality teaching, student success, strong relationships with Māori and Indigenous communities. Our work is grounded in relationships and in our responsibilities to mana whenua, Ngāi Tūāhuriri, and Te Tiriti o Waitangi. Ngā Painga o UC | Why UC At UC, being part of our team means more than just a job. We are committed to supporting our kaimahi | staff’s career and wellbeing with benefits designed to help our kaimahi grow and feel connected, supported and valued. We offer a range of benefits, for more information, please visit us: https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/about-uc/work-at-uc/benefits-working-uc For more information about Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha | University of Canterbury, please visit https://www.canterbury.ac.nz/ ) The closing date for this position is: 11 November 2026 (midnight, NZ time) Applications will be reviewed as they are received, and interviews may take place before the close date. Pēhea te tono mai | How You Apply Applications for this position must be submitted on our careers website and should include the following: a cover letter which includes a 2–3-page statement outlining your teaching philosophy and experience a CV which includes details of your geological interests and teaching experience. Please note, we do not accept applications by email, however we are happy to answer your queries in relation to the application process, please forward these to WorkatUC@canterbury.ac.nz For further information specifically about the role, please contact: teena.henderson@canterbury.ac.nz to discuss the opportunities associated with this position. You must have Aotearoa New Zealand or Australian citizenship/permanent residency to be considered for this role. Please note, this is an onsite position. #LI-DNI Job Details Reference # 33919 Posted on 29 Oct 2026 Closes on 11 Nov 2026 23:55 Location(s) Christchurch Expertise Lecturer Job level(s) Academic Work type(s) Continuing (Permanent) part-time More details (document) Position description 1 Lecturer (Teaching and Administration).pdf Position description 2 Position description 3 Position description 4 Print Apply © University of Canterbury | Te Whare Wānanga o Waitaha Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 28 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    }
